{"name":"Ocean Spray","hex":"#005379","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ocean","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the large bodies of water separating the continents.","The continuous body of salt water covering a majority of the Earth's surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ocean"},{"term":"spray","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A fine, gentle, dispersed mist of liquid.","Something resembling a spray of liquid."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To project a liquid in a dispersive manner toward something.","To project in a dispersive manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spray"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sea spray","blocks":[{"text":"Sea spray consists of aerosol particles formed from the ocean, primarily by ejection into Earth's atmosphere through bursting bubbles at the air-sea interface. Sea spray contains both organic matter and inorganic salts that form sea salt aerosol (SSA). SSA has the ability to form cloud condensation nuclei (CCN) and remove anthropogenic aerosol pollutants from the atmosphere. Coarse sea spray has also been found to inhibit the development of lightning in storm clouds."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Sea_spray_and_waves_%28Unsplash%29.jpg/330px-Sea_spray_and_waves_%28Unsplash%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea_spray","wikidata":"Q1046888"},{"title":"Ocean Spray (cooperative)","blocks":[{"text":"Ocean Spray Cranberries, Inc. is an American agricultural cooperative of cranberry growers headquartered in Plymouth County, Massachusetts. It currently has over 700 member growers. The cooperative employs about 2,000 people, with sales of $1.2 billion in fiscal year 2013 and accounts for 70% of North American cranberry production. Their products include cranberry sauce, fruit juices, fruit snacks, and dried cranberries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Ocean_Spray_logo.svg/330px-Ocean_Spray_logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean_Spray_(cooperative)","wikidata":"Q3348672"},{"title":"Ocean","blocks":[{"text":"The ocean is the body of salt water that covers approximately 70.8% of Earth. The ocean is conventionally divided into large bodies of water, which are also referred to as oceans, and are themselves mostly divided into seas, gulfs, and subsequent bodies of water. The ocean contains 97% of Earth's water and is the primary component of Earth's hydrosphere, acting as a huge reservoir of heat for Earth's energy budget, as well as for its carbon cycle and water cycle, forming the basis for climate and weather patterns worldwide. The ocean is essential to life on Earth, harboring most of Earth's animals and protist life. It originated photosynthesis and therefore Earth's atmospheric oxygen, and still supplies half of it."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg/330px-Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean","wikidata":"Q9430"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Sea_spray_sea_foam_connection.png/500px-Sea_spray_sea_foam_connection.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Sea_spray_sea_foam_connection.png/1280px-Sea_spray_sea_foam_connection.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Sea_spray_sea_foam_connection.png/500px-Sea_spray_sea_foam_connection.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Connection between sea foam and sea spray formation. The dark orange line indicates processes common to the formation of both sea spray and sea foam.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASea_spray_sea_foam_connection.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Ocean_mist_and_spray_2.jpg/500px-Ocean_mist_and_spray_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Ocean_mist_and_spray_2.jpg/1280px-Ocean_mist_and_spray_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/Ocean_mist_and_spray_2.jpg/500px-Ocean_mist_and_spray_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Sea spray containing marine microorganisms can be swept high into the atmosphere where they become aeroplankton. These airborne microorganisms may travel the globe before falling back to earth.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOcean_mist_and_spray_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/OceanSprayCranberriesBabcockWisconsinWIS173WIS80.jpg/500px-OceanSprayCranberriesBabcockWisconsinWIS173WIS80.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/OceanSprayCranberriesBabcockWisconsinWIS173WIS80.jpg/500px-OceanSprayCranberriesBabcockWisconsinWIS173WIS80.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Processing plant near Babcock, Wisconsin, 2010","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOceanSprayCranberriesBabcockWisconsinWIS173WIS80.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Ocean_Spray_Tomah.jpg/500px-Ocean_Spray_Tomah.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/t